Subject: [PATCH] master: single-argument class_create() on RHEL 9 kernels that backported it

Building the master on Rocky/RHEL 9 fails with:

    master/module.c:115:26: error: passing argument 1 of 'class_create' from
      incompatible pointer type [-Werror=incompatible-pointer-types]
      115 |     class = class_create(THIS_MODULE, "EtherCAT");
    master/module.c:115:13: error: too many arguments to function 'class_create'

Mainline Linux 6.4 (commit 1aaba11da9aa, "driver core: class: remove
module * from class_create()") dropped the owner argument, leaving

    struct class *class_create(const char *name);

Upstream module.c already handles both forms, but selects between them
purely on LINUX_VERSION_CODE:

    #if LINUX_VERSION_CODE < KERNEL_VERSION(6, 4, 0)

Red Hat backported the new signature into their 5.14 kernel while keeping
the version number frozen for kABI stability.  The version test therefore
says "older than 6.4" and picks the two-argument call, against a kernel
that declares the one-argument form.  This is the same class of failure as
0001-cdev-vm_flags-const-on-rhel9.patch, and the same fix applies: narrow
the version test with a RHEL_RELEASE_CODE exclusion.

Upstream carries no RHEL guards anywhere in the tree, though it does carry
SUSE ones for exactly this kind of backport -- see devices/ccat/netdev.c:914
and devices/8139too-5.14-ethercat.c:2397.  The idiom is established; only
the distro is missing.

THRESHOLD -- READ THIS BEFORE TRUSTING 9.6

The 9.6 threshold below is NOT verified across el9 minors.  It is verified
only on 9.8, where the one-argument form is definitely present.  If you are
on an el9 older than 9.8, check which form your kernel declares:

    grep -n 'class_create' \
      /usr/src/kernels/$(uname -r)/include/linux/device/class.h

    class_create(const char *name);                 -> one argument, patch needed
    class_create(struct module *owner, const char *name);  -> two, lower the
                                                              threshold or drop
                                                              this patch

Getting it wrong is loud, not silent: too few or too many arguments is a
hard compile error, never a runtime surprise.

Note devices/ccat/module.c:75 has the identical construct and would need the
identical change.  It is left alone deliberately -- ccat is not enabled by
this site's configure line, and the patch set stays as small as the build
requires.

Applies to: IgH EtherCAT master, tag 1.6.12
Verified on: Rocky 9.8, kernel 5.14.0-687.10.1.el9_8.0.1.x86_64
Not fixed upstream as of stable-1.6 (650888c5).
---
 master/module.c | 4 +++-
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/master/module.c b/master/module.c
--- a/master/module.c
+++ b/master/module.c
@@ -111,7 +111,9 @@
         }
     }
 
-#if LINUX_VERSION_CODE < KERNEL_VERSION(6, 4, 0)
+#if LINUX_VERSION_CODE < KERNEL_VERSION(6, 4, 0) \
+    && !(defined(RHEL_RELEASE_CODE) \
+         && RHEL_RELEASE_CODE >= RHEL_RELEASE_VERSION(9, 6))
     class = class_create(THIS_MODULE, "EtherCAT");
 #else
     class = class_create("EtherCAT");
